From: Oskari Rauta <oskari.rauta@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] isvp-t41: add a built-in default environment for the T41NQ

A board whose environment sector is blank or corrupt has to boot from the
environment compiled into u-boot, because at that point there is no Linux and
so no fw_setenv to repair it with. Without this the T41NQ reaches the u-boot
prompt and stops there.

Names and structure follow OpenIPC's own bootloaders rather than the vendor's,
so a user's muscle memory carries over: baseaddr rather than loadaddr, bootargs
behind a setargs indirection, uknor16m / urnor16m to update over tftp, and
clroverlay to wipe the overlay.

Nothing user-specific, network-specific or unit-specific is included. ethaddr in
particular must be absent: XBURST_MAC_FROM_EFUSE derives a stable per-unit MAC
from the SoC's eFUSE chip serial, and it yields to an environment that already
provides one. wlandev is left out for the same class of reason - it names the
wifi driver, which varies between T41 boards, and setting it also moves eth0 off
DHCP onto a static fallback address.

u-boot's own .env mechanism is used rather than buildroot's
BR2_TARGET_UBOOT_DEFAULT_ENV_FILE: the file then lives in the source tree and is
version controlled with everything else. The buildroot option points at an
absolute path, and when that path is a generated file the environment is
silently dropped on the next clean build - which is exactly what happened while
this port was being developed.

The file is selected by CONFIG_ENV_SOURCE_FILE="isvp-t41nq" rather than being
named after SYS_BOARD, so the other T41 variants sharing board/ingenic/isvp-t41
are unaffected.

Signed-off-by: Oskari Rauta <oskari.rauta@gmail.com>
---
 board/ingenic/isvp-t41/isvp-t41nq.env | 157 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 157 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 board/ingenic/isvp-t41/isvp-t41nq.env

diff --git a/board/ingenic/isvp-t41/isvp-t41nq.env b/board/ingenic/isvp-t41/isvp-t41nq.env
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/board/ingenic/isvp-t41/isvp-t41nq.env
@@ -0,0 +1,157 @@
+# Built-in default environment for the T41NQ (GC5603, 16 MiB SPI NOR).
+#
+# This is what the board boots with when its environment sector is blank or has
+# been erased - a fresh flash, or a bad write to the env block. Everything
+# needed to reach a shell has to be here, because at that point there is no
+# Linux and so no fw_setenv to repair it with.
+#
+# It is installed with CONFIG_ENV_USE_DEFAULT_ENV_TEXT_FILE, which REPLACES
+# U-Boot's generated default environment rather than adding to it. That is
+# deliberate: the generated one also carries bootdelay, loadaddr, mtdids and
+# usb_ignorelist, none of which this board uses and two of which would be empty.
+# What is listed below is therefore the whole environment, with nothing behind
+# it. bootdelay and baudrate are safe to omit because the code falls back to
+# their Kconfig values (common/autoboot.c, common/board_f.c).
+#
+# Note this file is NOT preprocessed - it is read by filechk_defaultenv.h, which
+# simply drops lines starting with '#' and empty lines. So comments are '#', not
+# C style, and ${...} stays literal.
+#
+# Names and structure follow OpenIPC's bootloaders so a user's muscle memory
+# works: baseaddr, bootargs behind a setargs indirection, uknor16m / urnor16m.
+#
+# Offsets are measured from a working camera, not assumed. They do not match
+# OpenIPC's Goke layout and cannot: Goke puts the env at 0x40000 behind a 256k
+# boot partition, and this u-boot image is ~0x41800 bytes, which does not fit in
+# 256k. Hence a 320k boot partition and everything after it shifted up.
+#
+#   0x000000  320k   boot          SPL + u-boot
+#   0x050000   64k   env           this environment
+#   0x060000  2240k  kernel        uImage.t41
+#   0x290000  8192k  rootfs        rootfs.squashfs.t41
+#   0xA90000  5568k  rootfs_data   overlay
+#                    ------------
+#                    16384k        exactly 16 MiB
+
+# console
+baudrate=115200
+serialport=ttyS1
+panic_timeout=2
+
+# Memory split. osmem/rmem/nmem are what the kernel is given; totalmem is what
+# OpenIPC's userspace reports and several of its scripts read. 74+46+8 = 128.
+osmem=74M@0x0
+rmem=46M@0x4a00000
+nmem=8M@0x7800000
+totalmem=128M
+
+# baseaddr is OpenIPC's name for the DRAM scratch buffer images are loaded into
+# before being written to flash, and the address the kernel is read to before
+# bootm. OpenIPC's bootloaders have no loadaddr, so there is none here either -
+# one name for one address, with nothing to drift out of step. U-Boot's own net
+# commands read "loadaddr" when no address is given, but every command below
+# passes one explicitly.
+flash_len=0x1000000
+baseaddr=0x80600000
+kern_addr=0x60000
+kern_size=0x230000
+
+# The partition holding the overlay must be called exactly "rootfs_data":
+# general/overlay/init greps /proc/mtd for that name and usr/sbin/sysupgrade
+# looks its targets up the same way. Called anything else the jffs2 mount fails
+# and the overlay silently falls back to tmpfs - every setting is then lost on
+# reboot, with nothing in the log to say why.
+#
+# "jz_sfc" is not a name we choose either: the mtd-id must equal the name the
+# kernel driver already gave the MTD, or cmdlinepart matches nothing and the
+# chip comes up unpartitioned. OpenIPC's ingenic_sfc driver calls it jz_sfc.
+mtdparts=jz_sfc:320k(boot),64k(env),2240k(kernel),8192k(rootfs),5568k(rootfs_data)
+
+# boot - init=/init is OpenIPC's own overlay setup script
+root=/dev/mtdblock3
+rootfstype=squashfs
+init=/init
+bootargs=mem=${osmem} rmem=${rmem} nmem=${nmem} console=${serialport},${baudrate}n8 panic=${panic_timeout} root=${root} rootfstype=${rootfstype} init=${init} mtdparts=${mtdparts} ${extras}
+bootcmd=setenv setargs setenv bootargs ${bootargs}; run setargs; sf probe; sf read ${baseaddr} ${kern_addr} ${kern_size}; bootm ${baseaddr}
+
+# Boot a development rootfs over NFS instead, the way OpenIPC's boards do.
+nfsroot=/srv/nfs/t41nq
+bootargsnfs=mem=${osmem} rmem=${rmem} nmem=${nmem} console=${serialport},${baudrate}n8 panic=${panic_timeout} root=/dev/nfs rootfstype=nfs ip=${ipaddr}:::255.255.255.0::eth0 nfsroot=${serverip}:${nfsroot},v3,nolock rw ${extras}
+bootnfs=setenv setargs setenv bootargs ${bootargsnfs}; run setargs; tftpboot ${baseaddr} uImage.${soc}; bootm ${baseaddr}
+
+# soc and sensor are what load_ingenic reads to pick its modules, and soc also
+# selects the image names below. It is "t41", not "t41nq", because SOC_MODEL
+# names the driver directory and the module names shared by every T41 SKU; the
+# board-specific part lives in the defconfig name and the device tree. A blank
+# environment must still name both, or the camera comes up with no video and
+# nothing explaining why.
+soc=t41
+sensor=gc5603
+
+# This board's LEDs and IR-cut, as plain GPIOs.
+gpio_default=81o 82o
+gpio_ir850=81
+gpio_ircut=49 50
+gpio_white=82
+
+# Update over tftp. OpenIPC's names, this board's offsets.
+#
+# "&&" between every step, unlike Goke's environment which separates erase from
+# write with ';'. A failed erase must not be followed by a write, and a failed
+# tftp must not be followed by either - that is the one way this sequence can
+# kill a camera that was working a moment earlier.
+#
+# "sf update" rather than "sf erase" + "sf write": it erases, writes and
+# verifies in one step, skips blocks that already match, and prints
+# "Updating, N%" as it goes (cmd/sf.c). A multi-second flash write is otherwise
+# completely silent, with no way to tell a slow write apart from a hang - and on
+# a bootloader write that is exactly when you want to see progress.
+#
+# It takes the PARTITION length rather than ${filesize}, which is what makes the
+# mw.b prefill load-bearing: it puts 0xff behind the image so the tail of the
+# partition ends up erased rather than holding whatever DRAM contained.
+uknor16m=mw.b ${baseaddr} ff 1000000; tftpboot ${baseaddr} uImage.${soc} && sf probe && sf update ${baseaddr} ${kern_addr} ${kern_size}
+urnor16m=mw.b ${baseaddr} ff 1000000; tftpboot ${baseaddr} rootfs.squashfs.${soc} && sf probe && sf update ${baseaddr} 0x290000 0x800000
+
+# uunor16m rewrites the bootloader that is currently running, so it is the one
+# command here that can leave the board unbootable. Run dunor16m first and keep
+# the copy: with the case closed there is no other way back.
+uunor16m=mw.b ${baseaddr} ff 1000000; tftpboot ${baseaddr} u-boot-with-spl-lzma.bin.${soc} && sf probe && sf update ${baseaddr} 0x0 0x50000
+
+# Read a partition back out to the tftp server BEFORE overwriting it. On an
+# assembled camera this is the only backup route there is: no SOIC programmer,
+# and the single USB port is permanently occupied by the wifi card.
+dknor16m=sf probe && sf read ${baseaddr} ${kern_addr} ${kern_size} && tftpput ${baseaddr} ${kern_size} kernel-backup.bin
+drnor16m=sf probe && sf read ${baseaddr} 0x290000 0x800000 && tftpput ${baseaddr} 0x800000 rootfs-backup.bin
+dunor16m=sf probe && sf read ${baseaddr} 0x0 0x50000 && tftpput ${baseaddr} 0x50000 uboot-backup.bin
+
+# Deliberately absent, and each for a reason:
+#
+#   ethaddr           MUST NOT be set here. misc_init_r() in
+#                     arch/mips/mach-xburst/ethaddr.c derives a stable per-unit
+#                     MAC from the SoC's eFUSE chip serial - the same derivation
+#                     Linux uses, so bootloader and OS agree and DHCP
+#                     reservations survive the hand-off. Its first line is "a
+#                     MAC provisioned in the environment always wins": set
+#                     ethaddr here and the eFUSE is never consulted, and every
+#                     camera flashed with this image shares one address.
+#   wlandev           names the wifi driver, so it is per unit: this bootloader
+#                     is for T41 boards in general and they do not all carry the
+#                     same card. It also changes ethernet - S40network runs a
+#                     plain "ifup eth0" when wlandev is unset, but takes the
+#                     wireless path when it is set, and that path gives eth0 a
+#                     static ${netaddr_fallback:-192.168.2.10} instead of a DHCP
+#                     lease. Worst case the named driver is not the card that is
+#                     fitted: wlan0 never comes up either and the camera is on no
+#                     reachable network at all, which on an assembled unit means
+#                     opening the case to get back in.
+#   wlanssid, wlanpass
+#                     credentials, and per-installation.
+#   ipaddr, serverip, netmask, gatewayip, netaddr_fallback
+#                     user- and network-specific; set them by hand before using
+#                     tftp or NFS.
+#   an overlay-wipe   a one-liner to erase the rootfs_data partition is handy
+#                     while testing, but it is a personal convenience rather
+#                     than something a shipped bootloader should carry - and one
+#                     wrong offset in it erases the wrong partition. Add it per
+#                     unit with fw_setenv if you want it.
